
<p>Ana Swanson and Devlin Barrett, reporting yesterday for The New York Times (gift link):</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>Investigators at the Commerce Department were deep into a
monthslong investigation of a Singaporean company that they
suspected of smuggling Nvidia’s artificial intelligence chips
into China when their work came to a sudden halt late last year.
The investigation centered on Megaspeed, a data center company
led by an executive who U.S. officials now believe to be a
Chinese spy who had a relationship with Nvidia’s chief executive,
Jensen Huang.</p>

<p>But the case was abruptly transferred to the Justice Department
last year. The shift came after Commerce Department investigators
working on the case received an email from Nvidia’s general
counsel seeking information about the inquiry into Megaspeed.</p>

<p>It is not necessarily unusual for Commerce Department agents
working on a case to speak with a company’s representatives about
issues involving their firm. But what was unusual was the content
of the message. Tim Teter, Nvidia’s general counsel, said that
Jeff Kessler, a top official at the department, had given Nvidia
permission to reach out about the case.</p>

<p>The agents were alarmed that Nvidia had invoked the name of Mr.
Kessler, their superior, who was nominated to his role by
President Trump. Some viewed Mr. Kessler’s interaction with Nvidia
as violating government procedure and potentially tainting the
case, according to current and former government officials and
others familiar with the incident.</p>
</blockquote>

